---
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import ComponentPreview from '../../components/ComponentPreview.astro';

const npmCmd = `mkdir my-talk
cd my-talk
npm init -y
npm install rikiki-deck
npx rikiki init index.html`;

const minDeck = `<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="rikiki/tokens.css">
  <script type="module" src="rikiki/dist/index.js"></script>
</head>
<body>
  <deck-root>
    <deck-cover brand="my-talk">
      <h1>Hello, <span class="accent">world</span>.</h1>
    </deck-cover>
    <deck-feature eyebrow="One slide">
      <h1 slot="title">That's all you need.</h1>
      <deck-md>
        Write **markdown**, drop &lt;deck-*&gt; blocks, navigate with arrows.
      </deck-md>
    </deck-feature>
  </deck-root>
</body>
</html>`;

const cdnDeck = `<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/rikiki-deck@0.7.2/tokens.css">
<script type="module" src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/rikiki-deck@0.7.2/dist/index.js"></script>`;

const cloneCmd = `git clone https://gitlab.com/tordu-jardin/rikiki.git
cd rikiki/rikiki
npm install
npm run build`;

const serve = `# Any static server works · for example:
npx serve .
# ES modules require http://; do not double-click the file`;

const deliver = `# measure the deck in a real browser
npx rikiki check index.html

# one PDF page per slide
npx rikiki export index.html`;

const minDeckPreview = `<deck-cover brand="my-talk">
  <h1>Hello, <span class="accent">world</span>.</h1>
</deck-cover>
<deck-feature eyebrow="One slide">
  <h1 slot="title">That's all you need.</h1>
  <deck-md>Write **markdown**, drop &lt;deck-*&gt; blocks, navigate with arrows.</deck-md>
</deck-feature>`;
---

<Docs
  title="Getting started"
  eyebrow="Quickstart"
  description="For a first deck: install, write one slide, serve the folder, then check and export it. Four steps, no build, no toolchain. Or hand the whole thing to an agent."
>

<h2 id="agent">Or let an agent do it</h2>
<p>
  Two commands, then ask in plain words. The skills carry the compositions, the
  editorial rules and the order to try fixes in; the agent writes the HTML,
  runs <code>rikiki check</code>, and fixes what comes back.
</p>
<CodeBlock
  label="terminal"
  lang="sh"
  code={`npm i rikiki-deck
npx rikiki skills   # installs 3 skills into .claude/skills/`}
/>
<p>
  Not using Claude Code? Point whatever you use at
  <code>node_modules/rikiki-deck/docs/llms/rikiki-workflow.md</code> · the
  brief-to-deck path written for an agent, which assumes nothing beyond a shell
  and a browser. The rest of this page is the same job by hand.
</p>

<h2 id="install">1 · Install</h2>
<p>
  Pick the install style that fits your workflow.
</p>

<h3 id="install-cdn">Via CDN (no install, needs the network)</h3>
<p>
  Add two lines to any HTML page. Zero tooling · but the deck fetches the
  framework at load time, so it needs a working connection to jsdelivr every
  time it runs. For an offline or archived deck, install from npm or bundle to
  a single file instead. Always pin a version, as below.
</p>
<CodeBlock label="<head>" lang="html" code={cdnDeck} />

<h3 id="install-npm">Via npm (versioned, runs from your own folder)</h3>
<CodeBlock label="terminal" lang="js" code={npmCmd} />
<p>
  The command writes <code>index.html</code> and copies its assets into
  <code>rikiki/</code> inside <code>my-talk/</code>.
</p>
<p>
  One thing still leaves the machine: the default theme
  (<code>tokens.css</code>, which imports <code>themes/rikiki.css</code>) pulls
  its typefaces from Google Fonts. For a deck that must run offline, pass
  <code>--theme siliceum</code> to <code>init</code> · that theme's woff2 files
  are copied into <code>rikiki/fonts/</code> · or accept the system-font
  fallback, which is what <code>rikiki bundle</code> produces when it strips the
  remote import.
</p>

<h3 id="install-clone">Via git clone (contributors)</h3>
<p>
  The npm package lives in the <code>rikiki/</code> folder of the repository.
  Build it from the TypeScript sources only if you plan to change the
  framework itself.
</p>
<CodeBlock label="terminal" lang="js" code={cloneCmd} />

<h2 id="first-deck">2 · Your first deck</h2>
<p>
  Edit the generated <code>index.html</code>. This minimal example uses the assets copied by <code>init</code>.
</p>
<CodeBlock label="my-talk/index.html" lang="html" code={minDeck} />
<ComponentPreview code={minDeckPreview} full />
<p>
  <code>rikiki/tokens.css</code> is the theme entry point: it imports
  <code>themes/rikiki.css</code>, so linking either file loads the same default
  theme. Pages here use both spellings.
</p>

<h2 id="serve">3 · Serve it</h2>
<p>
  Modules need <code>http://</code> · don't double-click the file. Run any
  static server from the <code>my-talk/</code> folder:
</p>
<CodeBlock label="terminal" lang="js" code={serve} />

<p>
  Open <code>http://localhost:7799/</code> and press <code>→</code> to
  go through the slides. That's the loop.
</p>

<h2 id="check">4 · Check and deliver</h2>
<p>
  Before a deck leaves your machine, measure it and print it. The first
  command loads the deck in a real browser and reports what is wrong with it,
  slide by slide; the second turns it into a PDF, one page per slide. Both
  need the optional <code>playwright</code> peer.
</p>
<CodeBlock label="terminal" lang="js" code={deliver} />
<p>
  <a href="/docs/cli">Check and deliver</a> covers the rest: the exit codes
  <code>check</code> returns, the JSON report a script can read, the pictures
  <code>render</code> writes, and the single-file bundle.
</p>

